INV.HLP - help texts of the RETROINV full-screen client.

Each section starts with a line "@TOPIC" and ends at the next such
line.  Lines before the first section, like these, are not shown.
Lines hold at most TUI_HELPW characters (TUI.C), the help box's
text width; longer ones are cut.  A section holds at most
TUI_HELPLINES lines (TUI.C).  The build (TEST/hlpchk.py) puts an index
of the sections before these lines, through which the client reads a
section with one read.
@LOGIN
Logging in

Type your user name and press Enter, then your password and Enter.
The password is not shown while you type it.  User names are upper
case; the password is checked exactly as typed.

After three failed attempts the session ends and the program stops.
An account that must change its password goes to the password screen
first.

The first time: the sample data has the users ADMIN, OPER and READER;
a new, empty data directory has the one user ADMIN.  The first
password is the user name in lower case (admin, oper, reader); a new
one is asked for at the first login.

Esc or F3 leaves the program.
@HOME
The home screen

It shows who is logged in, the access level, today's date, and how
many items, locations and categories the inventory holds.  A date
before 1990 means the PC's clock was never set: set it with the DOS
command DATE, as loans, reservations and service records take today's
date.  With no items yet, Items > New item adds the first; Locations >
New location adds the rooms, shelves and boxes to keep them in.

  F10          open the menu bar
  Alt-letter   open the menu of that letter
  Ctrl-F       search the items
  F3           quit (asks first)
  F9           the keys every screen has
@MENU
The menu bar

  Left, Right  the previous or next menu
  Up, Down     move between the entries
  Enter        choose the selected entry
  letter       choose the entry of that letter
  Esc, F10     close the menu

Entries drawn dimmed need a higher access level than yours; choosing
one says so.  A click on a title opens that menu, on an entry chooses
it; a click elsewhere closes the menu.  The last menu, Record, holds
what the screen shown can do with its records, each entry naming its
key when it has one.
@ITEMS
Item lists

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Enter        open the selected item
  Ins          a new item (level 2)
  Ctrl-E       edit the selected item (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected item, after asking (level 2)
  Ctrl-D       duplicate the selected item and edit the copy; Esc
               before any save deletes the copy again, unless its
               notes, tabs or a split were opened (level 2)
  Ctrl-L, Ctrl-R, Ctrl-U   lend the selected item, reserve it or
               put it in use: fill in the form (level 2)
  Esc          back

The Record menu (Alt-C) holds these and also:
  Move         move the selected item, with its parts, to a location
               chosen from the list (level 2)
  Install in   install the selected item in another (level 2): type
               the other's ID, or text to choose it from a list
  Take out     take the selected item out of its assembly, to a
               location chosen from the list (level 2)

Items > Browse by shows the list by ID, by location (the items of no
location first, then those of each location in the order of
Locations), by maker or by serial; the status line names the order.
Works says whether the item works: yes, no, intrm (intermittently) or
? (not tested).  The Location column names the place where each item
is; it is empty for an item whose location is not known.  Status says
why an item is not available: On loan, Reserved or In use (all its
units), Installed, Disposed or None left; it is empty for an available
item.  While a long list is filled, Esc stops it with the rows found
so far.  An assembly is an item with other items installed in it as
its parts, such as a PC with its cards and drives: Items > Assemblies
lists those not installed themselves.  Items > Categories lists the
categories an item can have.
@ITEM
The item form

  Tab, Down          next field         Shift-Tab, Up   previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter, Ctrl-S      save               Esc             cancel
  F4                 pick list for the category, location, tags and
                     the fields with fixed choices
  Space              step through the choices of such a field
  Ctrl-Y             clear it, when it may be empty (Condition)

  Ctrl-E             edit, from view mode (level 2)

The Record menu (Alt-C) shows the item's Parts, Service records,
Loans and holds, or what is Used with it, in the panel below the
first fields (Esc there comes back to the form), and Split units
takes units off into a new item at the same location (level 2).

Location: choose it with F4 from the list of locations, or type its
code or number; the line below shows where it is, such as Office >
Shelf A > Bin 12.  Leave it empty when the place is not known.

Status: A active, D disposed of.  Condition, the looks alone: NEW
new, EXC excellent, GD good, FR fair, PR poor, or empty, not graded.
Works: ? not tested (a new item's and a copy's), Y yes, I works
intermittently, N no; only you set it.  Note a test in Service.

Tags: words of your own, such as retro or a project, between commas or
spaces; each 1 to 12 of A-Z 0-9 - _ . / +.  F4 lists the tags in use
(at most 50, A to Z; Esc while they load lists those found so far):
Enter adds the one chosen after a comma.  A save shows the tags as
kept: one comma between them, a repeat left out.

Dates are typed as YYYY-MM-DD.  F4 on the Notes field opens the notes
of a saved item.  The Record menu works in view mode, or while editing
as long as nothing has been changed; save with Enter first otherwise.
A split leaves the item at least one unit and those lent, reserved or
in use.  Beside the quantity are the units on loan, reserved and in
use, how many are still available, and how many items are used with
this one (Used with it lists them); a line too long for its place
ends in ~.  Parent
shows the item an installed part is in: its ID, maker and model; a
part is always at its assembly's location.  A part opened with Enter
in the Parts tab is shown in this form; Esc, or a save, goes back to
the item before it.
@MEMO
The notes editor

  arrows, Home, End, PgUp, PgDn     move
  Ctrl-Home, Ctrl-End               first, last line
  Enter        split the line       Backspace, Del   join lines
  Ins          insert or overwrite  Ctrl-Y           delete the line
  Ctrl-S       save                 Esc, F3          back (asks if
                                                     changed)
@LOCS
Locations

Every place things are kept, as a tree: rooms, and inside them
shelves, racks, boxes or bins, down to eight levels.  Each location is
listed under the one it is inside, indented, with its type, its code
and its capacity.  Items can be kept at any level.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  letter       the next location whose name starts with it
  Enter        the items at the selected location
  Ins          a new location inside the selected one (level 2); in
               an empty list, at the top level
  Ctrl-E       edit the selected location (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected location, after asking (level 2);
               only a location without items and without locations
               inside it can be deleted
  Esc          back

The Record menu (Alt-C) holds these and Move, which moves the selected
location, with everything inside it, into another chosen from the
list, or to the top level (level 2).  A location pick list opens on
the current location, but in a very long tree its search may stop
short: Enter on the row where it stopped then asks before choosing it.

Locations > New location, or a new location whose Inside is left
empty, adds one at the top level.  To find the items whose location is
not known, use Search with Location set to "(no location)".
Locations > Location types lists the types a location can have.
@LOC
The location form

  Tab, Down          next field         Shift-Tab, Up   previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter, Ctrl-S      save               Esc             cancel
  F4                 pick list for the type and for Inside

  Name       what the location is called; two locations inside the
             same one cannot have the same name
  Type       Room, Shelf, Box, ... (F4 lists them), its name shown
             beside it; a new location starts with the type of the
             others in the same place (Room at the top level), and
             takes that of its new place while Type is unchanged
  Inside     the location it is in, chosen with F4 or typed as its
             code or number; empty for the top level.  The line below
             shows its place.  Changing it moves the location with
             everything inside it; a location cannot go inside itself,
             nor inside one at the deepest level.
  Code       optional: a short code for labels and typing, such as
             B12 (a letter, then letters, digits or -)
  Capacity   optional: how much it holds, a whole number

F4 on the Notes field opens the notes of a saved location.
@CATS
Categories

The categories an item can have, in their order: code, name and order
number.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Ins          a new category (level 2)
  Enter, Ctrl-E  change the selected category's name or order number
               (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected category, after asking (level 2);
               only one that no item has can be deleted
  Esc          back

The code is given once, when the category is made: up to four letters
and digits.  The list is sorted by the order number, 0 to 99 (a new
one without it takes 50), then by code.
@LTYPES
Location types

The types a location can have, such as Room, Shelf, Box or Bin, in
their order: code, name and order number.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Ins          a new type (level 2)
  Enter, Ctrl-E  change the selected type's name or order number
               (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected type, after asking (level 2); only
               one that no location has can be deleted
  Esc          back

The code is given once, when the type is made: up to eight letters and
digits.  The list is sorted by the order number, 0 to 99 (a new one
without it takes 50), then by code.
@CONTENTS
Contents of a location

The items at one location; the title shows where it is.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Enter        open the selected item
  Ins          a new item at this location (level 2)
  Ctrl-E       edit the selected item (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected item, after asking (level 2)
  Ctrl-D       duplicate the selected item and edit the copy; Esc
               before any save deletes the copy again, unless its
               notes, tabs or a split were opened (level 2)
  Ctrl-L, Ctrl-R, Ctrl-U   lend the selected item, reserve it or
               put it in use: fill in the form (level 2)
  Esc          back to the locations

The Record menu (Alt-C) holds these, Move, Install in and Take out as
in the item lists, and two switches: Not installed only, all the
items, installed parts included, or only those not installed in
another; Everything below, the items of this location only, or also
those of every location inside it, at any depth.

A location that holds other locations, such as a room with its
shelves, first shows the items of every location inside it too; one
that holds none, its own.  The status line says what is shown; the
Location column names where each item is.  While a long list is
filled, Esc stops it with the rows found so far.
@SEARCH
Search

Fill in the fields and press Enter:

  Text         finds the text in maker, model, description, tags,
               serial number and type, among the items the filters
               given keep
  Serial       finds an exact serial number (when no text is given;
               the filters do not apply)
  Category, Location, Status, Available, Tags, Works
               alone, list the items that match all the filters given

Location keeps the items at that location only, or with All below Y
also those of every location inside it; F4 lists the locations, and
its first line, (no location), finds the items whose place is not
known.  Available is Y or N; Y keeps the items that can be lent,
reserved or used: status A, not installed in another, and not all on
loan, reserved or in use; N, like blank, filters nothing.  Y already
means status A, so Status stays empty with it.  Space steps Status
through A and D and back to empty.  Tags keeps the items that carry
every tag given, whole (net is not network; the Text finds parts of
words); F4 adds one from the tags in use.  Works keeps the items in
that state: ? not tested, Y, I (intermittently), N, or Y?I, all but
the broken ones; empty keeps all.  The results are an item list; Esc
there returns to the search form with its values kept.  F4 gives the
pick lists of the filter fields.  Esc, Ctrl-F and F10 leave the search
form at once, without asking; F3 asks only whether to quit.
@SETTINGS
Settings

These are kept in the settings file.  The data directory, the station
name and BIOS output take effect the next time the program starts; CGA
snow avoidance at once, the monochrome palette from the next screen,
the printer settings from the next report.

  Printer      LPT1, LPT2 or LPT3 (the parallel ports), or PRN, the
               DOS printer device, for a printer redirected with MODE
               or on a network
  Type         EPSON for an Epson-compatible printer (bold headings,
               the page length sent to the printer), PLAIN for any
               other printer: text only
  Page lines   lines of paper per page (66 for 11-inch paper at 6
               lines per inch)
  Label        the label width in characters, x the lines from one
               label to the next (35 x 6 for 3 1/2 by 15/16 inch
               labels on one-inch pitch)

A number outside its limits is refused, and the message gives them.

  Tab, Down          next field         Shift-Tab, Up   previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter, Ctrl-S      save               Esc             cancel
  F4                 pick list for Printer and Type
  Space              step through their choices
@PASSWD
Changing the password

Type the old password, then the new one twice.  A password is made of
printable characters, without spaces, and must differ from the old
one; one too short or too long is refused, and the message gives the
lengths allowed.  None of them is shown while you type.

  Tab, Down          next field         Shift-Tab, Up   previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter, Ctrl-S      change             Esc             cancel

When the change is required after logging in, Esc logs out and F3
quits, each after asking.
@SERVICE
Service records

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Enter        open the selected record
  Ins          a new record for the item (Service tab, level 2)
  Ctrl-E       edit the selected record (level 2)
  Del          delete the selected record, after asking (level 2)
  Esc          back (from the Service tab: to the item form)

Items > Service lists the records newest added first.  The Service
tab of an item lists its records by service date, oldest first, those
without a date before the others.
@SERVREC
The service record form

  Tab, Down          next field         Shift-Tab, Up   previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter, Ctrl-S      save               Esc             cancel
  F4                 pick list for the type
  Space              step through the types

Types: RP repair, CL clean, RC recap, TS test, MD modify, IN inspect.
Type and summary are required.  Dates are typed as YYYY-MM-DD; a new
record left without a date gets today's.  F4 on the Notes field opens
the notes of a saved record.  In view mode Ctrl-E switches to editing,
and the Record menu's Open item shows the record's item when the form
was opened from Items > Service.
@PARTS
Parts of an item

An assembly is an item with other items installed in it as its parts,
such as a PC with its cards and drives.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Enter        open the selected part in the item form
  Ins          install an item in this one (level 2): type its ID, or
               text to choose it from a list
  Esc          back to the item form

The Record menu (Alt-C) holds these and Take out, which takes the
selected part out of its assembly, to a location chosen from the list
(level 2).

The list shows every part below the item, each part followed by its
own parts, indented one step per level.  A part keeps its own parts
when it is installed or taken out, and an installed part always lies
at the location of the item it is built into.  An item on loan,
reserved or in use cannot be installed, nor one that would end up
holding itself, nor one that would make the assembly too deep or too
large; the message says which.
@LOANS
Loans and holds

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Enter        open the selected row
  Ins          new: choose Lend..., Reserve... or Use with..., then
               type the item's ID, or text to choose it among the
               available items, and fill in the form; in the Loans
               tab, for this item (level 2)
  Ctrl-L, Ctrl-R, Ctrl-U   new, as Ins, without the choice
  Ctrl-E       edit the selected row (level 2)
  Ctrl-PgDn, Ctrl-PgUp   the next or previous view: open rows of every
               kind, open loans, open reservations, in use, overdue,
               all rows; in the Loans tab, this item's rows or what
               is used with it
  Esc          back (from the Loans tab: to the item form)

The Record menu (Alt-C) holds these and also:
  Return or release   return a loan, or release a reservation or use:
               type the date as YYYY-MM-DD, or leave it empty for
               today (level 2)
  Open item    show the selected row's item (not in the item's own
               Loans tab)

A row takes units of an item out of stock: lent to a borrower,
reserved (for a purpose, a person or another item) or in use with
something (another item, such as a monitor with a PC).  The list
starts with the open rows, the earliest due first.  A loan or a
reservation still open after its due date is overdue: its row is
marked with ! and highlighted.  The Loans tab of an item lists its
rows, open and ended, in the order they were made; Ctrl-PgDn shows the
open rows of the items reserved for it or in use with it, where Ins
adds one for an item asked for, with this item as its host, and Open
item shows the selected row's item in the item form (Esc comes
back).  A row
that names an item but no holder shows For item n (a reservation) or
With item n (a use) as its holder, left blank among the rows that
item hosts.
@LOAN
The loan, reservation and use forms

  Tab, Down          next field         Shift-Tab, Up   previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter, Ctrl-S      save               Esc             cancel
  F4                 on For item / With item: choose it from a list

A loan needs a borrower and a due date.  A reservation needs whom or
what it is for, or the item it is for; Until is optional.  A use needs
what it is used with, or the item, and has no due date.  Type the
item's ID, or text and F4 to choose it from the items holding it (text
left there is chosen when the form is saved); its maker and model show
after it.  Dates are typed as YYYY-MM-DD; a new row without a date out
starts today.  The quantity (1 unless changed) and the date out can be
set only on a new row, and no more units can be taken than the item
has available.  An item that is disposed of or installed in an
assembly cannot be lent, reserved or used.  Return or release in the
list ends a row.
In view mode Ctrl-E switches to editing.
@REPORTS
Reports

Fill in the options and press Enter; the form stays open for another
run.

  Order        item listing: by location (grouped, with the items and
               the quantity of each location), by ID, or by maker
  Category, Location
               only the items of that category or location; F4 gives
               the pick lists
  All below    Y: also the items of every location inside the one
               given; the report then goes by location
  Status       item listing: ALL, A (active) or D (disposed of)
  Works, Tags  listing, valuation and item labels: only the items in
               that works state and with every tag given, as in
               Search (Works ? lists the untested stock); F4 on Tags
               lists the tags in use
  Overdue, As of
               loans and holds: only the loans and reservations due
               before the date; the date marks the late ones with !
               and heads the pages (empty: today)
  Output       SCREEN (shown in the viewer, the first choice),
               PRINTER (the printer of the settings) or FILE (the text
               a plain printer would get, into the file named; an
               existing file is replaced only after asking)

By location, each location has a heading with its place, such as
Office > Shelf A, in the order of the location list; the items whose
place is not known come first.  The valuation lists the active items
by location with the price paid and the value, subtotals per location,
the total, and totals by category.  The amounts are each item's own,
added as stored: they are not multiplied by the quantity.

While a report runs, the status line counts the pages asked for and
the records.  Esc stops it: the report then ends with a line saying
so, and prints no totals.  A printer that is not ready asks Retry or
Cancel; Cancel drops the rest of the report.  The printer, its type
(EPSON or PLAIN) and the page length are set in Settings; set an Epson
printer to its IBM character table, so that the characters of the
code page print as on screen.
@LABELS
Labels

Item labels: one label per item, with its ID and category, maker and
model, serial number and location.  Type an item ID for one label;
otherwise the labels of all items, or of the category and location
given (with All below, also of the locations inside it), in the order
of the location list.

Bin labels: one label per location, with its name in double width,
its code if it has one, its place (such as Office > Shelf A > Bin 12)
and its type.  Give a location for its label, with All below Y also
for every location inside it; leave it empty for all locations.

  Output       PRINTER, FILE or SCREEN, as for reports
  F4           the pick lists of category, location and tags
  Works, Tags  item labels: as in Search

Labels are printed on continuous stock, one label across.  The label
width and the pitch (lines from one label to the next) are set in
Settings.  Line the first label up by hand before printing.  Sheets of
labels with several across, as for a laser printer, are not laid out:
print to a FILE and arrange it with another program.
@VIEWER
The report viewer

  Up, Down     one line
  PgUp, PgDn   one screen
  Home, End    the first or the last screen
  Esc          close

It shows the report as a plain printer would print it; each page end
is a line across.  Of a very long report only the first lines are
shown, as the message on opening says.
@EXPORT
Export to a CSV file

Writes every record of a table to a text file of comma-separated
values, one line per record, the way dBASE III Plus writes with
COPY TO file DELIMITED.  Notes are not written.

  Table        ITEMS, LOCS, CATS, LTYPES (the location types), MAINT,
               LOANS or AUDIT; F4 lists them
  File         the file to write, on the machine that holds the data:
               the server, or this machine when working locally
  Field names  Y writes the field names as the first line

Enter starts the export, and the status line counts the records
written.  Esc stops it; the file is then left as it was, since it gets
its name only once complete.  What was written so far stays on the
machine that holds the data, under the file's name with an extension
starting &C.  A file that exists already is replaced only after you
agree.  Export and import need access level 3.
@IMPORT
Import a CSV file

Adds the lines of a text file of comma-separated values to ITEMS or
LOCS as new records, checked as a new item or location typed in a
form would be.  Nothing that exists is changed or deleted.

  Table        ITEMS or LOCS (F4 lists them)
  File         the file to read, on the machine that holds the data
  Field names  Y when the first line names the columns; otherwise the
               columns are the table's fields in their order,
               without the notes

ITEMS: ITEM_ID CATEGORY ITEMTYPE MFR MODEL SERIALNO DESCR COND QTY
ON_LOAN RESERVED IN_USE ATTACHED LOC_ID PARENT_ID ACQ_DATE ACQ_SRC
ACQ_PRICE CUR_VALUE TAGS STATUS CREATED UPDATED.  LOCS: LOC_ID
PARENT_ID LOC_NAME LOC_CODE LTYPE CAPACITY.  Dates are YYYYMMDD;
CATEGORY, COND and STATUS take the codes of the item form, LTYPE a
location type's code.

A value in quotes is kept as it is, with "" standing for one quote.
An empty value takes the default of a new record.  The columns the
program fills in itself (item ID, on loan, reserved, in use, attached,
the assembly, the dates created and updated) are ignored, so an
exported file can be imported back: the records get new IDs.  An
item's LOC_ID takes a location's number or its code.  A location's
PARENT_ID takes a code, naming a location already kept or one on any
line of the file, or a number: when the file has a LOC_ID column
(every export, and every file without field names) the number names
the line of the file with that LOC_ID, otherwise a location already
kept.  A location may come before or after the ones inside it.

Every line is checked before anything is added.  If any line is
refused, nothing is added, and the refused lines are listed with their
reasons.  A disk error while the records are being added can stop the
import with some of them added; the screen says how many.  The import
needs the system to itself: other users must log out first.  A large
file takes minutes on a slow machine.  Export and import need access
level 3.
@PICKITEM
Choosing an item

The items holding the text typed in their maker, model, description,
tags, serial number or type, or every item when nothing was typed; for
a new loan, reservation or use only the available items, for the item
a row is reserved for or used with every active item.

  Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move through the list
  Enter        choose the selected item
  Esc          back, to type something else
@USERS
The users

Each user logs in with a name and a password and has an access
level: 1 may only look, 2 may also change the inventory, 3 may also
manage the users and the data files.  The list shows them in name
order; Status is A for active and D for disabled, and a disabled
user's row is marked.  Chg is T while the user must change the
password at the next login.

  Enter, Ctrl-E  edit the selected user
  Ins          a new user
  Del          delete the selected user, after asking
  Esc          back

The last active user of level 3 can be neither deleted, disabled nor
given a lower level.  Only users of level 3 can open this list.
@USER
The user form

  Tab, Down          next field         Shift-Tab, Up   previous field
  PgUp, PgDn         first, last field
  Enter, Ctrl-S      save               Esc             cancel
  Space, F4          the next level or status, or the list of them

The user name, of letters, digits and _ as many as the field holds,
is given when the user is created and cannot be changed afterwards.
A new user needs a level and a password and must change the password
at the first login.  Set the status to D to disable a user, who can
then no longer log in, and back to A to enable the user again.

To give a user a new password, type it twice; the user must change it
at the next login.  Left empty, the password stays as it is.  A
password is made of printable characters, without spaces; one too
short or too long is refused.  It is not shown while you type.
Changes of level and status take effect at the user's next login.
@ADMIN
Backup, restore, pack and reindex

These work on the data files as a whole and need the system to
themselves: other users must log out first.  Each can take minutes
when there is much data.  A directory named here is one on the machine
that holds the data: the server, or this machine when working locally.

  Backup    copies every table, notes file and index into a directory
            that exists already; a backup there before is replaced
  Restore   replaces all the data with the backup in a directory,
            after asking; the indexes are rebuilt from its tables
  Pack      removes the deleted records from the files for good,
            after asking, and rebuilds every index
  Reindex   rebuilds every index from its table, as after a damaged
            index

Each is written to the audit log.  They need access level 3.
@AUDIT
The audit log

Every change to the data is written to the audit log: who made it
(user and station), when, the table, the record, the field, and its
old and new value.  Logins, failed logins, password changes and the
work on the data files are there too.  The list shows the newest rows
first; with a table it shows that table's rows by record, oldest
first.  Failed logins are marked.

  Table        ITEMS, LOCS, CATS, LTYPES, USERS, MAINT, LOANS, or
               AUDIT for the work on the data files
  Record       the record's ID (for CATS and LTYPES its number in
               the file); it needs a table
  Station      the station the changes came from

  Enter        show the list with these filters; empty ones filter
               nothing
  Esc          in the list, back to the filters; here, leave

Values longer than their column are cut.  Packing renumbers the
records of CATS and LTYPES; rows written before it keep the old
numbers.
The audit log needs access level 3.
@SESSIONS
The sessions

The sessions open on the server, yours included and drawn bright:
the station, the user, the access level, the state, and the seconds
since the session's last request.  A station that has not logged in
shows no user and level 0.  F5 asks the server again.

Only a server has sessions: working locally, the screen says this is
not available.  It needs access level 3.
@KEYS
Keys everywhere

  F1           help
  F9           these keys, also in the help
  F10          the menu bar
  Alt-letter   the menu of that letter
  Ctrl-F       search
  F3           quit
  Esc          back, cancel
  Ctrl-Break   as Esc
  Alt-C        the Record menu: what the screen does with its records
  F5           in a list: fetch its rows again

F10, Alt-letter, Ctrl-F, F3 and a click on the menu bar work in lists,
on the home screen, and in a form in view mode or while nothing has
been changed (option forms and the viewer always).

In a list: Enter opens the row, Ins adds one, Ctrl-E edits it, Ctrl-D
duplicates it and Del deletes it, each as the screen allows; the
Record menu has the rest.

In a form: Enter or Ctrl-S saves, Tab and Down go to the next field
you can change (one only shown, such as Created, is passed), F4 gives
a field's pick list or opens the notes, and Ctrl-E turns a form you
are viewing to editing.

In a field: its value shows marked as you come to it, and what you
type replaces it; Left, Right, Home or End first changes it in place.
Then Left, Right, Home, End, Backspace, Del, Ins (insert or
overwrite) and Ctrl-Y (clear the field).

With a mouse driver loaded, the mouse works too:
  click        a menu title or entry, a list row, a form field, or a
               key named on the bottom line, as if pressed
  double click a list row: open it (as Enter)
  right button as Esc
